>
> Why would bones files be rendered incompatible by giving names to
> monsters? It's the same function that allows you to descend into a
> bones level and...
>
> Fido bites! You die...
>
> As opposed to "The little dog bites! You die..."
>
> Or am I missing some hack that gets stuck into mextra to make sure
> no one name is ever re-used? Even that would be ok so long as it
> didn't mess up older versions of code that read mextra.

Yup, its a hack, but not that one. Originally, IIRC, when you ran across
a named monster it would say soemthing like "Sam the Quantum Mechanic
hits!" That was too verbose, I guess, so it was shortened to "Sam hits!"
This isn't too confusing when it is a monster that you have named. But
when a random monster shows up and it says "Sam hits!" a bit of confusion
is not unlikely. So they removed the pre-named quantum mechanics.

I get around that by making unknown names. You will continue to get "The
Quantum Mechanic hits!" until you #chat with one, and the QM introduces
itself, or until you focus on it with telepathy (using ; or / ).
The way I store unknown names, though, is a hack to avoid any new data
fields, which would have ugly results when someone picked up the file
through hearse.

So I'd have to yank unknown names before saving the bonesfile.

That brings up another question, for me at least. Why do named monsters
retain their names in a bones file. Ghosts and Shopkeepers I can
understand, but upon entering a bones level, I shouldn't know that the
large dog used to be called Fido, right?

